---
import TreatmentPage from "../../../components/TreatmentPage.astro";

const T = "/services/fertility-treatment";

const page = {
  slug: "male-infertility",
  name: "Male Infertility Treatment",
  title: "Male Infertility Treatment in Lahore | Dr. Kamran Saleem",
  description:
    "Male infertility treatment in Lahore with Dr. Kamran Saleem: low sperm count, azoospermia, varicocele and infections. Book a confidential consultation.",
  eyebrow: "Male Infertility Treatment",
  h1: "Male Infertility Treatment in Lahore",
  waText: "Hello, I would like to book a confidential male fertility consultation with Dr. Kamran Saleem.",
  ctaLabel: "Book a confidential consultation",
  heroSub: [
    "Male infertility is often treatable, and the right treatment depends on the cause.",
    "At Kamran Fertility Clinic in Lahore, Dr. Kamran Saleem assesses sperm health, hormones and genital-tract infections in complete confidence, treats what can be treated, and explains clearly when surgery or assisted treatment such as IUI, IVF or ICSI is the better option.",
  ].join(" "),
  leadIn: [
    { rich: ["If you already have a semen analysis, bring it. If not, see our guides to the ", ["male fertility test", "/resources/male-fertility-test"], " and ", ["understanding a semen analysis", "/resources/understanding-a-semen-analysis"], "."] },
  ],
  sections: [
    {
      id: "your-doctor",
      title: "Your Doctor: Dr. Kamran Saleem",
      intro: "Postgraduate training in genito-urinary medicine and venereology, relevant to male reproductive health.",
      icon: "user",
      body: [
        "Dr. Kamran Saleem holds an MBBS and a postgraduate Diploma in Genito-Urinary Medicine and Venereology (UK). Some causes of male infertility involve the genito-urinary system, including infections, inflammation, blockages and problems with sexual function, and his training is relevant to assessing these conditions.",
        "Dr. Kamran leads your assessment and treatment plan. Where surgery or a specialist procedure is needed, such as varicocele repair or sperm retrieval, it is performed by an experienced urological surgeon and coordinated with your treatment cycle.",
      ],
    },
    {
      id: "male-infertility-treatment-options",
      title: "Male Infertility Treatment by Cause",
      intro: "There is no single treatment for every man. The plan depends on what your tests show.",
      icon: "bolt",
      body: [
        { ul: [
          "Low sperm count: treating the underlying cause, reviewing medicines and lifestyle, and assisted treatment such as IUI or IVF with ICSI when appropriate",
          "Hormonal problem: targeted hormone treatment when a specific deficiency is confirmed",
          "Infection: targeted treatment, only when tests or clinical findings support it",
          "Varicocele: repair in selected men, performed by a urological specialist",
          "Medicines or substances affecting fertility: reviewing prescribed medicines, anabolic steroids or external testosterone with a doctor",
          "Blocked ducts: surgical correction or sperm retrieval, followed by ICSI",
          "No sperm in the semen: finding out whether the cause is a blockage or reduced production, then sperm retrieval and ICSI where suitable",
          "Erectile or ejaculation problems: assessment and treatment of the underlying cause",
        ] },
        "When no clear cause is identified, the condition may be described as unexplained or idiopathic male infertility. Management depends on the couple’s overall fertility findings and may involve lifestyle advice, further evaluation or assisted reproductive treatment.",
        "Antioxidant and supplement courses are widely marketed, but the evidence for them is mixed. We avoid long, costly supplement regimes that are unlikely to help.",
      ],
    },
    {
      id: "low-sperm-count-treatment",
      title: "Low Sperm Count Treatment in Lahore",
      intro: "A low sperm count does not mean you cannot conceive. What matters is how low the count is and what is causing it.",
      icon: "flask",
      body: [
        "Depending on the findings, treatment may include:",
        { ul: [
          "Treating a hormonal condition, infection or varicocele that is affecting sperm production",
          "Stopping or reviewing medicines, anabolic steroids or external testosterone",
          "Stopping smoking, losing excess weight and avoiding prolonged heat on the testes",
          "IUI or IVF with ICSI may be considered, depending on the post-preparation sperm count and motility, the female partner’s fertility and other clinical factors",
        ] },
        "The goal is to address the underlying problem and help you and your partner understand the most appropriate route towards pregnancy.",
      ],
    },
    {
      id: "hormonal-treatment-for-male-infertility",
      title: "Hormonal Treatment for Male Infertility",
      intro: "Hormones such as FSH, LH and testosterone control sperm production.",
      icon: "refresh",
      body: [
        "When a specific deficiency is behind poor sperm production, targeted treatment can help. For example, some men with hypogonadotropic hypogonadism may be offered gonadotropin therapy under specialist supervision.",
        "Not every man with a low testosterone result needs the same medicine, and testosterone itself is not a fertility treatment. Testosterone injections, gels and supplements can shut down the signals that make sperm and may reduce sperm numbers sharply. If you are trying to conceive, speak to a doctor before you start them.",
      ],
    },
    {
      id: "infection-related-male-infertility",
      title: "Infection-Related Male Infertility",
      intro: "Some infections of the genital tract can affect fertility, and they are worth checking.",
      icon: "shield",
      body: [
        "Infections or inflammation such as urethritis, prostatitis and epididymitis can affect fertility in some men. Past sexually transmitted infections can also leave scarring that blocks sperm passage.",
        { ul: [
          "A review of symptoms and history",
          "Urine, semen or swab tests where relevant",
          "Targeted treatment when an infection is confirmed or clinically suspected",
          "Follow-up to check the response",
        ] },
        "Antibiotics are not a treatment for every semen problem. Taking them without a confirmed reason is unlikely to help and can cause harm, so treatment is based on clinical findings. Because this is Dr. Kamran’s area of training, these questions can be discussed privately and without embarrassment.",
      ],
    },
    {
      id: "varicocele-treatment",
      title: "Varicocele Treatment and Male Fertility",
      intro: "Many men with a varicocele are fertile and need no treatment.",
      icon: "heart",
      body: [
        "A varicocele is an enlargement of the veins around the testicle. It can raise testicular temperature and, in some men, reduce sperm quality. Repair is considered when:",
        { ul: [
          "The varicocele can be felt on examination",
          "Semen results are abnormal",
          "There is a fertility goal, and the couple’s overall situation and timeline support it",
        ] },
        "Repair is performed by a urological specialist. Semen results can take several months to improve afterwards, and surgery does not guarantee a pregnancy. We discuss the likely benefit and the alternatives before you decide.",
      ],
    },
    {
      id: "azoospermia-and-sperm-retrieval",
      title: "Azoospermia and Sperm Retrieval",
      intro: "No sperm in the ejaculate does not always mean no sperm.",
      icon: "flask",
      body: [
        "Azoospermia means no sperm in the ejaculated semen. It can be obstructive, where sperm are made but blocked, or non-obstructive, where sperm production is impaired. Hormone tests, ultrasound and sometimes genetics help tell which.",
        { ul: [
          "PESA: percutaneous epididymal sperm aspiration",
          "TESA: testicular sperm aspiration",
          "TESE and micro-TESE: testicular sperm extraction, including the microsurgical technique",
        ] },
        "In selected men, sperm can be retrieved by one of these procedures and used for ICSI as part of an IVF cycle. Retrieval is performed by an experienced urological surgeon, and Dr. Kamran coordinates it with your treatment cycle. It is not successful in every man, and the chance depends on the cause, so counselling before any procedure is essential.",
        { rich: ["See our ", ["ICSI treatment", `${T}/icsi`], " page for how retrieved sperm are used."] },
      ],
    },
    {
      id: "assisted-treatment-for-male-factor",
      title: "IUI, IVF or ICSI: Which Treatment Fits?",
      intro: "The degree of sperm impairment, the female partner’s fertility and your treatment history decide which option is realistic.",
      icon: "compare",
      body: [
        { ul: [
          "IUI: prepared sperm are placed in the uterus around ovulation. It may be considered with mild sperm impairment, open fallopian tubes and enough good-quality sperm after preparation",
          "IVF: eggs and sperm are combined in the laboratory and an embryo is transferred. It may be considered with female-factor problems, or when simpler treatment has not worked",
          "ICSI: a single sperm is injected into an egg during IVF. It may be considered with moderate to severe sperm impairment, very low counts, surgically retrieved sperm or previous fertilisation failure",
        ] },
        "ICSI is a technique used within IVF. It is not needed for every abnormal semen analysis, and we will tell you plainly when a simpler option is reasonable.",
        { rich: ["Read more about ", ["IUI", `${T}/iui`], ", ", ["IVF", `${T}/ivf`], " and ", ["ICSI", `${T}/icsi`], ", or see our wider guide to ", ["male infertility treatment", "/resources/male-infertility--treatment"], "."] },
      ],
    },
    {
      id: "tests-for-male-infertility",
      title: "Tests: What to Expect at Your First Consultation",
      intro: "A first visit is a conversation, not a procedure.",
      icon: "document",
      body: [
        "Dr. Kamran reviews your history, medicines and any previous reports, then advises which tests are actually worth doing, because not every man needs every test. Depending on your history, tests may include:",
        { ul: [
          "Semen analysis: sperm count, movement and shape, interpreted against WHO reference limits (6th edition, 2021). A single abnormal result is usually repeated to confirm it, because sperm numbers vary.",
          "Hormone tests: FSH, LH, testosterone and prolactin",
          "Scrotal ultrasound with Doppler, to look for varicocele or testicular abnormalities",
          "Infection screening of urine, semen and sexually transmitted infections where relevant",
          "Genetic testing for severely low counts or absent sperm",
          "Sperm DNA fragmentation testing, in selected cases",
        ] },
        { rich: ["More detail is in our ", ["Assessment & Diagnosis", "/services/assessment"], " service, including ", ["semen analysis", "/services/assessment/semen-analysis"], "."] },
      ],
    },
    {
      id: "improving-sperm-health",
      title: "How Long Does It Take to Improve Sperm Health?",
      intro: "Sperm production takes approximately three months, so changes need time to show.",
      icon: "refresh",
      body: [
        { ul: [
          "Stop smoking and avoid recreational drugs",
          "Do not use anabolic steroids or testosterone supplements unless prescribed, since they can shut down sperm production",
          "Limit alcohol",
          "Move toward a healthy weight and exercise regularly",
          "Avoid prolonged heat on the testes, such as hot baths, saunas and laptops on the lap",
          "Manage stress and sleep",
        ] },
        "Because sperm production takes approximately three months, changes in sperm health may take several months to appear in a follow-up semen analysis. Your doctor will advise when repeat testing is appropriate. Lifestyle changes support reproductive health but cannot correct every cause, so a medical cause, if found, should be treated as well.",
      ],
    },
    {
      id: "when-to-see-a-doctor",
      title: "When Should You See a Male Fertility Specialist?",
      intro: "Do not wait for your partner to be tested first.",
      icon: "heart",
      body: [
        "Consider an assessment if you have been trying for a year without pregnancy (six months if your partner is 35 or older), or earlier if you have:",
        { ul: [
          "A previous abnormal semen analysis",
          "A history of undescended testes, testicular surgery, torsion or injury",
          "Past genital infection, mumps or sexually transmitted infection",
          "Erectile or ejaculation difficulties, or reduced desire",
          "Testicular pain, swelling or a lump",
          "Chemotherapy, radiotherapy or long-term medication",
          "Use of anabolic steroids or testosterone",
        ] },
      ],
    },
    {
      id: "confidential-care",
      title: "Confidential, Respectful Care",
      intro: "Many men delay getting help because of embarrassment. There is no reason to.",
      icon: "shield",
      body: [
        "Assessment is private, discussed without judgment, and focused on practical answers. Reports are given to you and shared only with your consent.",
      ],
    },
    {
      id: "fees",
      title: "Fees",
      intro: "Costs depend on the tests and treatment you need.",
      icon: "document",
      body: [
        "Message the clinic on WhatsApp and the consultation fee will be confirmed before you visit.",
      ],
    },
    {
      id: "consultation-locations",
      title: "Consultation Locations in Lahore",
      intro: "Dr. Kamran Saleem sees patients at three Lahore locations.",
      icon: "user",
      body: [
        { ul: [
          "Rasheed Hospital: Main Boulevard, DHA Defence, Lahore",
          "TAMC: M.M. Alam Road, Lahore",
          "Shahnaz Hospital: Shalamar Link Road, Lahore 54000",
        ] },
        { rich: ["See each location page: ", ["DHA Lahore", "/locations/dha-lahore"], ", ", ["M.M. Alam Road", "/locations/mm-alam-road-lahore"], " and ", ["Shalamar", "/locations/shalamar-lahore"], "."] },
      ],
    },
  ],
  faqs: [
    { q: "Can male infertility be treated?", a: "Often, yes. The outlook depends on the cause: infections and hormonal problems can be treated directly, a varicocele can be repaired in selected men, and IUI, IVF with ICSI or surgical sperm retrieval can help when sperm remain impaired. Some causes cannot be reversed, but options for having a biological child often still exist." },
    { q: "What is a normal sperm count?", a: "In the WHO 6th edition manual (2021), the lower reference limits are about 16 million sperm per millilitre and about 42% total motility. Other measures such as sperm shape and total count also matter, and these are reference limits rather than a pass-or-fail mark. A result below them does not mean you cannot conceive, and a result above them does not guarantee fertility, so the full report is interpreted alongside your history." },
    { q: "Can low sperm count be treated without IVF?", a: "Sometimes. If a treatable cause such as an infection, a hormonal problem or a varicocele is found, treating it may improve results. Mild impairment may also suit IUI. When the post-preparation sperm count is low or other fertility factors are present, IVF with ICSI may be considered, and your doctor will explain why." },
    { q: "What is the treatment for azoospermia?", a: "Treatment depends on whether the cause is a blockage or reduced sperm production. In selected men, sperm can be retrieved from the epididymis or testicle with PESA, TESA or micro-TESE and used for ICSI. Retrieval is not successful in every case, so counselling beforehand is important." },
    { q: "Does a varicocele always need surgery?", a: "No. Many men with a varicocele never need treatment. Repair is considered in selected men when the varicocele can be felt on examination, semen results are abnormal and there is a fertility goal. Surgery does not guarantee pregnancy, and semen improvement can take several months." },
    { q: "Can testosterone or steroids cause male infertility?", a: "Yes. Testosterone injections, gels, supplements and anabolic steroids can switch off the hormones that drive sperm production, sometimes severely. Stopping them under medical guidance often allows recovery, although this can take months. If you are trying to conceive, speak to a doctor before starting any testosterone product." },
    { q: "How long does it take to improve sperm count?", a: "It can take around three months. Sperm production takes approximately three months, so changes in sperm health may take several months to appear in a follow-up semen analysis. Your doctor will advise when repeat testing is appropriate, because sperm numbers also vary naturally from one sample to the next." },
    { q: "Is male infertility treatment confidential?", a: "Yes. Your results and discussions are private, and reports are given to you and shared only with your consent. Many men delay seeking help because of embarrassment, and consultations are designed to be respectful and free of judgement." },
  ],
  closingHeading: "Male Infertility Treatment in Lahore With Dr. Kamran Saleem",
  closingParas: [
    "At Kamran Fertility Clinic, Dr. Kamran Saleem assesses and treats male fertility concerns in Lahore, including semen analysis interpretation, hormone assessment and genito-urinary health.",
    "Where surgery or specialist procedures such as varicocele repair or sperm retrieval are needed, we coordinate with trusted specialists and explain what happens where.",
  ],
  resources: [
    { href: "/resources/male-infertility--treatment", category: "Fertility Treatment", title: "Male Infertility Treatment: Causes, Tests and Options" },
    { href: "/resources/male-fertility-test", category: "Male Fertility", title: "Male Fertility Test: What It Shows" },
    { href: "/resources/understanding-a-semen-analysis", category: "Male Fertility", title: "Understanding a Semen Analysis" },
  ],
  close: {
    heading: "Ready for Answers?",
    body: "Bring any previous semen analysis or hormone reports. If you have none, we will tell you which tests are worth doing first.",
    cta: "Book a Confidential Consultation",
  },
};
---

<TreatmentPage {...page} />
